 The distance $AB$ between two points in polar coordinates \(A = (r_1, \theta_1)\) and \(B = (r_2, \theta_2)\) can be found using the formula: \[
AB = \sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2 - 2r_1r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)}
\] Given \(r_1 = 3\), \(r_2 = 9\), and \(\theta_1 - \theta_2 = \frac{\pi}{2}\), we substitute these values into the formula. \[
AB = \sqrt{3^2 + 9^2 - 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 9 \cdot \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)} = \sqrt{9 + 81 - 54 \cdot 0} = \sqrt{90} = 3\sqrt{10}
\] </think> <answer> \(3\sqrt{10}\) </answer>

